  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM EDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nConvection did not survive into our area last night and the main\nenergy slipped south from eastern IA into south-central IL. As\nof current radar imagery our area remains precipitation free.\nThere is an area of scattered showers moving eastward across \nnorthern IL which may slip in here along the cold front later\nthis morning but any strong convection looks fairly unlikely\nacross our area today due to the poor diurnal timing of the\nboundary passage. Still think an isolated strong to severe \nstorm or two may still be possible for our far southeastern\nparts of the CWA (i.e. Lima, OH area) but again with the poor\ntiming the surface instability will be low to non-existent when\nthe boundary pushes through those areas. Effective bulk shear\nvalues along the boundary will run from 40-50 kts which if\nconvection can initiate some organization could be achieved.\nSPC does have a small portion of our CWA under a slight risk for\nsevere storms and a bit larger area of marginal today. The\nbiggest threat from these storms would be damaging gusts and\nheavy downpours. Hazardous marine conditions for the near shore\nand Lake Michigan beaches today through Thursday morning with\nthe more gusty northerly flow.\n\nAlso in the wake of the cold front we actually may see some \nlake effect clouds and showers develop off of southern Lake \nMichigan late tonight and into Wednesday with the cooler air \naloft and warmer lake temperatures in place. Lows on Wednesday \nmorning will be in the 50s while parts of Lake Michigan surface \nwater temperatures are currently in the lower 60s to lower 70s. \nNot expecting much of accumulation from any of the showers but \nwill be enough to keep areas near the lake damp on Wednesday. \nHigh temperatures on Wednesday through Friday will mainly be in \nthe 70s with a northwesterly flow aloft in place. This may also \nkeep some smoke in the air with that trajectory. Slightly warmer\nthis weekend with highs in the low to mid 80s as ridging over \nthe central CONUS starts to push a bit eastward into the region.\nDry conditions also otherwise for the area at least through \nSaturday night. A weak shortwave could allow for some \nshowers/storms by Sunday afternoon but moisture may be hard to \ncome by with the ridging pushing eastward and blocking any \nsouthern moisture. Will keep our eyes on that.",
